{"step_index":356,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T12:00:12Z","thinking":"**Extracting Connection Information**\n\nObserving the screenshot, connection details for Centreon MAP are displayed. Specifically, addresses for the Legacy server and Engine server are presented with associated port numbers. This appears to be configuration information related to the MAP extension within the Centreon environment.\n\n\n**Analyzing Network Reachability Implications**\n\nExamining the documentation and field descriptions clarifies the distinction between addresses used by Central and user browsers. The `MAP Engine server address` is intended for client access, while the address \"seen by Central\" likely serves backend communication. The current configuration using a private IP for the `MAP Engine server address` presents an immediate access problem for external users.\n\n\n**Deducing Connection Test Behavior**\n\nThe `MAP Engine server address` dictates external client connectivity, which uses DNAT to route from the public IP to a reverse proxy. Conversely, \"MAP server address as seen by Central\" is used for backend communications, including testing connection using a cURL request originating from the Central server. It is crucial to set the correct `MAP Engine server address` for external accessibility, which the test verifies.\n\n\n**Confirming Failure and its Source**\n\nThe \"Test connection to server\" failure arises because Central server initiates a cURL request to the external address, `https://109.62.64.18:9443`. This request is blocked by the firewall as hairpin NAT is not configured. This discrepancy is the source of the persistent error, confirming the initial hypothesis.\n\n\n**Evaluating Proposed Solution's Efficacy**\n\nThe provided solution proposes updating the `/etc/hosts` file and `MAP Engine server address`. This configuration aims to resolve external and internal connectivity issues simultaneously. It should allow external users to access through the public IP via DNAT to the reverse proxy, then to the internal MAP service. The Central server, by resolving `map.extranetclient...` to an internal address, will bypass the hairpin NAT issue, connecting directly to the reverse proxy.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"~/.ansible/kssh -u superuser 172.30.198.49 'cat /etc/hosts'","Cwd":"/home/koby","WaitMsBeforeAsync":3000,"toolAction":"Running command","toolSummary":"Check /etc/hosts on 172.30.198.49"}}]}
